{"abstract":"Expressive state-of-the-art separation logics rely on step-indexing to model semantically complex features and to support modular reasoning about imperative higher-order concurrent and distributed programs. Step- indexing comes, however, with an inherent cost: it restricts the adequacy theorem of program logics to a fairly simple class of safety properties. In this paper, we explore if and how intensional refinement is a viable methodology for strengthening higher-order concurrent (and distributed) separation logic to prove non-trivial safety and liveness properties. Specifically, we introduce Trillium, a language-agnostic separation logic framework for showing intensional refinement relations between traces of a program and a model. We instantiate Trillium with a concurrent language and develop Fairis, a concurrent separation logic, that we use to show liveness properties of concurrent programs under fair scheduling assumptions through a fair liveness-preserving refinement of a model. We also instantiate Trillium with a distributed language and obtain an extension of Aneris, a distributed separation logic, which we use to show refinement relations between distributed systems and TLA+ models.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Timany, Amin","Gregersen, Simon Oddershede","Stefanesco, Léo","Hinrichsen, Jonas Kastberg","Gondelman, Léon","Nieto, Abel","Birkedal, Lars"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-727911"],"filters":{"added":"2024-05","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Max Planck Institute for Software Systems"],"keyword":["Concurrency","Distributed systems","Formal verification","Higher order logic","Refinement","Separation logic"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"trillium higher order concurrent and distributed separation logic for intensional refinement","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3632851","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-727911","identifier":[{"id":"85181733834","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-906e414b-9f87-4cf5-8de7-8b051a77b3ed"],"keyword":["Distributed systems","concurrency","formal verification","higher-order logic","refinement","separation logic"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-0C4BBE1DE6C74501B22773D8A719018F"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3632851","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Natural Sciences, Department of Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"de","key":"max planck institute for software systems","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Max Planck Institute for Software Systems","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Amin","key":"timanypau","last":"Timany","locid":"e651b5c3-4642-4bcd-8108-224aa2ecfc08","name":"Timany, Amin","orcid":"0000-0002-2237-851X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Simon Oddershede","key":"gregersenpau","last":"Gregersen","locid":"136d0771-5abe-4843-913c-a06a8dc3a936","name":"Gregersen, Simon Oddershede","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Léo","key":"stefanescopau","last":"Stefanesco","name":"Stefanesco, Léo","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jonas Kastberg","key":"hinrichsenpau","last":"Hinrichsen","locid":"47107d73-8750-4515-ba0f-33c7ba993099","name":"Hinrichsen, Jonas Kastberg","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Léon","key":"gondelmanpau","last":"Gondelman","locid":"ddba9f3a-84f2-4e3a-a6a1-6f25ef2f8f3a","name":"Gondelman, Léon","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Abel","key":"nietopau","last":"Nieto","name":"Nieto, Abel","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Lars","key":"birkedalpau","last":"Birkedal","locid":"bd012524-7d01-45f4-9bbd-8f8273bf14c7","name":"Birkedal, Lars","orcid":"0000-0003-1320-0098","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3632851","issn":["24751421"],"journal":"Proceedings of the ACM on Programming Languages","paperid":"9","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the ACM on Programming Languages","volume":"8"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85181733834"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-906e414b-9f87-4cf5-8de7-8b051a77b3ed"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Trillium : Higher-Order Concurrent and Distributed Separation Logic for Intensional Refinement","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-727911","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"We introduce a linear concurrent separation logic, called LinearActris, designed to guarantee deadlock and leak freedom for message-passing concurrency. LinearActris combines the strengths of session types and concurrent separation logic, allowing for the verification of challenging higher-order programs with mutable state through dependent protocols. The key challenge is to prove the adequacy theorem of LinearActris, which says that the logic indeed gives deadlock and leak freedom \"for free\"from linearity. We prove this theorem by defining a step-indexed model of separation logic, based on connectivity graphs. To demonstrate the expressive power of LinearActris, we prove soundness of a higher-order (GV-style) session type system using the technique of logical relations. All our results and examples have been mechanized in Coq.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Jacobs, Jules","Hinrichsen, Jonas Kastberg","Krebbers, Robbert"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-727301"],"filters":{"added":"2024-05","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Netherlands"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Radboud University Nijmegen"],"keyword":["Coq","Iris","Message passing","Separation logic","Session types","deadlocks"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"deadlock free separation logic linearity yields progress for dependent higher order message passing","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3632889","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-727301","identifier":[{"id":"85182264413","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-4f3fd284-4cc4-491c-a6c4-f8d6365b35ee"],"keyword":["Coq","Iris","Message passing","deadlocks","separation logic","session types"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-6DBCFFDA0CF2BFBDCAB932A01C2B3D76"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3632889","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"nl","key":"radboud university nijmegen","loc":["Netherlands","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Radboud University Nijmegen","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Natural Sciences, Department of Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jules","key":"jacobspau","last":"Jacobs","name":"Jacobs, Jules","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Jonas Kastberg","key":"hinrichsenpau","last":"Hinrichsen","locid":"47107d73-8750-4515-ba0f-33c7ba993099","name":"Hinrichsen, Jonas Kastberg","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Robbert","key":"krebberspau","last":"Krebbers","name":"Krebbers, Robbert","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3632889","issn":["24751421"],"issue":"POPL","journal":"Proceedings of the ACM on Programming Languages","paperid":"47","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the ACM on Programming Languages","volume":"8"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85182264413"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-4f3fd284-4cc4-491c-a6c4-f8d6365b35ee"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Deadlock-Free Separation Logic : Linearity Yields Progress for Dependent Higher-Order Message Passing","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-727301","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"We introduce Multris, a separation logic for verifying functional correctness of programs that combine multiparty message-passing communication with shared-memory concurrency. The foundation of our work is a novel concept of multiparty protocol consistency, which guarantees safe communication among a set of parties, provided each party adheres to its prescribed protocol. Our concept of protocol consistency is inspired by the bottom-up approach for multiparty session types. However, by considering it in the context of separation logic instead of a type system, we go further in terms of generality by supporting new notions of implicit transfer of knowledge and implicit transfer of resources. We develop tactics for automatically verifying protocol consistency and for reasoning about message-passing operations in Multris. We evaluate Multris on a range of examples, including the well-known two- and three-buyer protocols, as well as a new verification benchmark based on Chang and Roberts's ring leader election protocol. To ensure the reliability of our work, we prove soundness of Multris w.r.t. a low-level channel semantics using the Iris framework in Coq.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Hinrichsen, Jonas Kastberg","Jacobs, Jules","Krebbers, Robbert"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-773238"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Hinrichsen, Jonas Kastberg"],"country":["Netherlands","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Radboud University Nijmegen","Weill Cornell Medical College"],"keyword":["Coq","Iris","Message passing","Separation logic","Session types"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"multris functional verification of multiparty message passing in separation logic","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3689762","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-773238","identifier":[{"id":"85206831445","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-a4019c66-d74b-4d65-a7e5-d72b2666c0a0"],"keyword":["Coq","Iris","Message passing","multiparty","separation logic","session types"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-A538DC5E83CF5DF966E7A7919AB3978B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3689762","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Natural Sciences, Department of Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"weill cornell medical college","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Weill Cornell Medical College","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"nl","key":"radboud university nijmegen","loc":["Netherlands","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Radboud University Nijmegen","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jonas Kastberg","key":"hinrichsenpau","last":"Hinrichsen","locid":"47107d73-8750-4515-ba0f-33c7ba993099","name":"Hinrichsen, Jonas Kastberg","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Jules","key":"jacobspau","last":"Jacobs","name":"Jacobs, Jules","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Robbert","key":"krebberspau","last":"Krebbers","name":"Krebbers, Robbert","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3689762","issn":["24751421"],"issue":"OOPSLA2","journal":"Proceedings of the ACM on Programming Languages","paperid":"322","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the ACM on Programming Languages","volume":"8"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85206831445"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-a4019c66-d74b-4d65-a7e5-d72b2666c0a0"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Multris : Functional Verification of Multiparty Message Passing in Separation Logic","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-773238","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"We present a foundationally verified implementation of a reliable communication library for asynchronous client-server communication, and a stack of formally verified components on top thereof. Our library is implemented in an OCaml-like language on top of UDP and features characteristic traits of existing protocols, such as a simple handshaking protocol, bidirectional channels, and retransmission/acknowledgement mechanisms. We verify the library in the Aneris distributed separation logic using a novel proof pattern - -dubbed the session escrow pattern - -based on the existing escrow proof pattern and the so-called dependent separation protocols, which hitherto have only been used in a non-distributed concurrent setting. We demonstrate how our specification of the reliable communication library simplifies formal reasoning about applications, such as a remote procedure call library, which we in turn use to verify a lazily replicated key-value store with leader-followers and clients thereof. Our development is highly modular - -each component is verified relative to specifications of the components it uses (not the implementation). All our results are formalized in the Coq proof assistant.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Gondelman, Léon","Hinrichsen, Jonas Kastberg","Pereira, Mário","Timany, Amin","Birkedal, Lars"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-693682"],"filters":{"added":"2023-11","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Portugal"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","NOVA University Lisbon"],"keyword":["Concurrency","Distributed systems","Formal verification","Higher order logic","Refinement","Separation logic"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"verifying reliable network components in a distributed separation logic with dependent separation protocols","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3607859","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-693682","identifier":[{"id":"85170638815","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-5b0ef8ae-4be0-4077-ab93-240870492250"],"keyword":["Distributed systems","concurrency","formal verification","higher-order logic","refinement","separation logic"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-BA708BB164F3ECBEB49847AE7EC2D4EC"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3607859","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.au.dk/ws/files/418358492/3607859.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Natural Sciences, Department of Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"pt","key":"nova university lisbon","loc":["Portugal","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"NOVA University Lisbon","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Léon","key":"gondelmanpau","last":"Gondelman","locid":"ddba9f3a-84f2-4e3a-a6a1-6f25ef2f8f3a","name":"Gondelman, Léon","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jonas Kastberg","key":"hinrichsenpau","last":"Hinrichsen","locid":"47107d73-8750-4515-ba0f-33c7ba993099","name":"Hinrichsen, Jonas Kastberg","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Mário","key":"pereirapau","last":"Pereira","name":"Pereira, Mário","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Amin","key":"timanypau","last":"Timany","locid":"e651b5c3-4642-4bcd-8108-224aa2ecfc08","name":"Timany, Amin","orcid":"0000-0002-2237-851X","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Lars","key":"birkedalpau","last":"Birkedal","locid":"bd012524-7d01-45f4-9bbd-8f8273bf14c7","name":"Birkedal, Lars","orcid":"0000-0003-1320-0098","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3607859","issn":["24751421"],"issue":"ICFP","journal":"Proceedings of the ACM on Programming Languages","pages":"847-877","paperid":"217","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the ACM on Programming Languages","volume":"7"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85170638815"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-5b0ef8ae-4be0-4077-ab93-240870492250"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Verifying Reliable Network Components in a Distributed Separation Logic with Dependent Separation Protocols","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-693682","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"We develop an account of dependent session protocols in concurrent separation logic for a functional language with message-passing. Inspired by minimalistic session calculi, we present a layered design: starting from mutable references, we build one-shot channels, session channels, and imperative channels. Whereas previous work on dependent session protocols in concurrent separation logic required advanced mechanisms such as recursive domain equations and higher-order ghost state, we only require the most basic mechanisms to verify that our one-shot channels satisfy one-shot protocols, and subsequently treat their specification as a black box on top of which we define dependent session protocols. This has a number of advantages in terms of simplicity, elegance, and flexibility: support for subprotocols and guarded recursion automatically transfers from the one-shot protocols to the dependent session protocols, and we easily obtain various forms of channel closing. Because the meta theory of our results is so simple, we are able to give all definitions as part of this paper, and mechanize all our results using the Iris framework in less than 1000 lines of Coq.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Jacobs, Jules","Hinrichsen, Jonas Kastberg","Krebbers, Robbert"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-693840"],"filters":{"added":"2023-11","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Netherlands"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Radboud University Nijmegen"],"keyword":["Concurrency","Message passing","Separation logic","Session types"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"dependent session protocols in separation logic from first principles functional pearl","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3607856","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-693840","identifier":[{"id":"85170645482","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-777d0384-79c4-43e7-8296-7261bd5ebed1"],"keyword":["concurrency","message passing","separation logic","session types"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-3735B1220B4A44F0A61B2F0A257967C8"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3607856","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.au.dk/ws/files/417855185/3607856.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"nl","key":"radboud university nijmegen","loc":["Netherlands","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Radboud University Nijmegen","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Natural Sciences, Department of Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jules","key":"jacobspau","last":"Jacobs","name":"Jacobs, Jules","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Jonas Kastberg","key":"hinrichsenpau","last":"Hinrichsen","locid":"47107d73-8750-4515-ba0f-33c7ba993099","name":"Hinrichsen, Jonas Kastberg","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Robbert","key":"krebberspau","last":"Krebbers","name":"Krebbers, Robbert","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3607856","issn":["24751421"],"issue":"ICFP","journal":"Proceedings of the ACM on Programming Languages","pages":"768-795","paperid":"214","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the ACM on Programming Languages","volume":"7"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85170645482"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-777d0384-79c4-43e7-8296-7261bd5ebed1"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Dependent Session Protocols in Separation Logic from First Principles (Functional Pearl)","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-693840","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Message passing is a useful abstraction for implementing concurrent programs. For real-world systems, however, it is often combined with other programming and concurrency paradigms, such as higher-order functions, mutable state, shared-memory concurrency, and locks. We present Actris: a logic for proving functional correctness of programs that use a combination of the aforementioned features. Actris combines the power of modern concurrent separation logics with a first-class protocol mechanism—based on session types— for reasoning about message passing in the presence of other concurrency paradigms. We show that Actris provides a suitable level of abstraction by proving functional correctness of a variety of examples, including a channel-based merge sort, a channel-based load-balancing mapper, and a variant of the map-reduce model, using concise specifications. While Actris was already presented in a conference paper (POPL’20), this paper expands the prior presentation significantly. Moreover, it extends Actris to Actris 2.0 with a notion of subprotocols—based on session-type subtyping—that permits additional flexibility when composing channel endpoints, and that takes full advantage of the asynchronous semantics of message passing in Actris. Soundness of Actris 2.0 is proven using a model of its protocol mechanism in the Iris framework. We have mechanised the theory of Actris, together with custom tactics, as well as all examples in the paper, in the Coq proof assistant.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Hinrichsen, Jonas Kastberg","Bengtson, Jesper","Krebbers, Robbert"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-260887"],"filters":{"added":"2022-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Netherlands"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Radboud University Nijmegen"],"keyword":["Actor model","Concurrency","Iris","Message passing","Session types"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"actris 000000000002 000000000000 asynchronous session type based reasoning in separation logic","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.46298/lmcs-18(2:16)2022","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-260887","identifier":[{"id":"85134740099","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-e66e1855-83f7-4b84-bdfc-3f410a41b164"],"keyword":["Iris","Message passing","actor model","concurrency","session types"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-995A6F59DA758D3AB4EB903335582547"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.46298/lmcs-18(2:16)2022","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Natural Sciences, Department of Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"nl","key":"radboud university nijmegen","loc":["Netherlands","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Radboud University Nijmegen","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Jonas Kastberg","key":"hinrichsenpau","last":"Hinrichsen","locid":"47107d73-8750-4515-ba0f-33c7ba993099","name":"Hinrichsen, Jonas Kastberg","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Jesper","key":"bengtsonpau","last":"Bengtson","name":"Bengtson, Jesper","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Robbert","key":"krebberspau","last":"Krebbers","name":"Krebbers, Robbert","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.46298/lmcs-18(2:16)2022","issn":["18605974"],"issue":"2","journal":"Logical Methods in Computer Science","publisher":"Logical Methods in Computer Science","status":"p","title":"Logical Methods in Computer Science","volume":"18"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85134740099"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-e66e1855-83f7-4b84-bdfc-3f410a41b164"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"ACTRIS 2.0 : ASYNCHRONOUS SESSION-TYPE BASED REASONING IN SEPARATION LOGIC","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-260887","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Session types—a family of type systems for message-passing concurrency—have been subject to many extensions, where each extension comes with a separate proof of type safety. These extensions cannot be readily combined, and their proofs of type safety are generally not machine checked, making their correctness less trustworthy. We overcome these shortcomings with a semantic approach to binary asynchronous affine session types, by developing a logical relations model in Coq using the Iris program logic. We demonstrate the power of our approach by combining various forms of polymorphism and recursion, asynchronous subtyping, references, and locks/mutexes. As an additional benefit of the semantic approach, we demonstrate how to manually prove typing judgements of racy, but safe, programs that cannot be type checked using only the rules of the type system.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Hinrichsen, Jonas Kastberg","Louwrink, Daniël","Krebbers, Robbert","Bengtson, Jesper"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382729"],"event":{"acronym":"CPP","title":"ACM SIGPLAN International Conference on Certified Programs and Proofs"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Netherlands"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Delft University of Technology","Radboud University Nijmegen","University of Amsterdam"],"keyword":["Coq","Iris","Message passing","Semantic typing","Separation logic","Session types"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"machine checked semantic session typing","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3437992.3439914","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382729","identifier":[{"id":"85100539579","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-618f45a0-50de-43b2-a610-fe8b52212bdd"],"keyword":["Coq","Iris","Message passing","Semantic typing","Separation logic","Session types"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-A0CECA95A56FA5B289E244A38EE66E7C"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85827344/Machine_Checked_Semantic_Session_Typing.pdf","version":"Other Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3437992.3439914","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Programming Logic and Semantics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"nl","key":"university of amsterdam","loc":["Netherlands","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Amsterdam","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"nl","key":"delft university of technology","loc":["Netherlands","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Delft University of Technology","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"nl","key":"radboud university nijmegen","loc":["Netherlands","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Radboud University Nijmegen","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jonas Kastberg","key":"hinrichsenpau","last":"Hinrichsen","name":"Hinrichsen, Jonas Kastberg","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Daniël","key":"louwrinkpau","last":"Louwrink","name":"Louwrink, Daniël","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3 4","corresponding":null,"first":"Robbert","key":"krebberspau","last":"Krebbers","name":"Krebbers, Robbert","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jesper","key":"bengtsonpau","last":"Bengtson","locid":"b2b68ce2-6257-45bf-ab48-dc17d05c885e","name":"Bengtson, Jesper","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3437992.3439914","pages":"178-198","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"CPP 2021: Proceedings of the 10th ACM SIGPLAN International Conference on Certified Programs and Proofs"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85100539579"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-618f45a0-50de-43b2-a610-fe8b52212bdd"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Machine-Checked Semantic Session Typing","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382729","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Session types and separation logic are two formalisms used to obtain strong guarantees about concurrent programs, each with their own benefits. Session types provide an intuitive protocol mechanism for specifying sequences of value exchanges, where each exchange is constrained to a specific type. In contrast, separation logic is often used for more involved specifications and proofs about shared-memory concurrency. This thesis combines the two efforts by introducing dependent separation protocols – a session-type based reasoning mechanism in separation logic – and a semantic session type system – a session type system that combines the expressivity of separation logic with the simplicity of type checking. Dependent separation protocols mimic the intuition behind session types, by describing sequences of value exchanges constrained by separation-logic propositions. The dependent separation protocols are formalised for a representative language with fine-grained concurrency, mutable state, and higher-order functions. This formalisation, which we call Actris, is built on top of the higher-order concurrent separation logic Iris. The use of Iris provides the ability to reason about recursion, substructural properties, and integration with existing concurrency models, such as shared-memory and lock-based concurrency. We demonstrate the expressivity of Actris by proving the correctness of several variants of a distributed merge sort algorithm, a distributed load-balancing mapper, and a variant of the map-reduce algorithm. The semantic session type system is achieved using semantic typing and logical relations, where the types and judgements of the type system are defined in terms of Actris. The type system supports term- and session-type polymorphism, equi-recursion, and subtyping, as well as copyable types, shared and mutable reference types, and mutex types. The type system additionally supports manual typing proofs, where typing judgements that cannot be derived from the typing rules of the type system can instead be proven in the logic. This technique effectively lets us combine the full expressivity of the logic, with the simplicity of the syntax-directed type checking of the type system. We demonstrate this feature by manually proving the typing judgement of a program, where two threads operate on a single session-typed channel endpoint in parallel. All of the results of the thesis have been mechanised in the interactive proof assistant Coq, on top of the existing mechanisation of Iris, along with tactic support for resolving proof obligations related to message passing in a style similar to working with session types.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Hinrichsen, Jonas Kastberg"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384716"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"und","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"sessions and separation","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"doi","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109067574/PhD_Thesis_final_version_Jonas_Kastberg_Hinrichsen.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384716","ids":["itu-e9e0e0fd-c6ba-484e-8ba5-253c4561e264"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-DCA8D0A7A6DACD366A8FF2E2837F6B44"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109067574/PhD_Thesis_final_version_Jonas_Kastberg_Hinrichsen.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Programming Logic and Semantics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jonas Kastberg","key":"hinrichsenpau","last":"Hinrichsen","name":"Hinrichsen, Jonas Kastberg","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"isbn":["9788779490475"],"issn":["16023536"],"issue":"177","pages":"156","publisher":"IT-Universitetet i København","series":"ITU-DS","status":"p"},"review":"Undetermined","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-e9e0e0fd-c6ba-484e-8ba5-253c4561e264"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Sessions and Separation","type":"Other-Internet Publication","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384716","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Message passing is a useful abstraction to implement concurrent programs. For real-world systems, however, it is often combined with other programming and concurrency paradigms, such as higher-order functions, mutable state, shared-memory concurrency, and locks. We present Actris: a logic for proving functional correctness of programs that use a combination of the aforementioned features. Actris combines the power of modern concurrent separation logics with a first-class protocol mechanism—based on session types—for reasoning about message passing in the presence of other concurrency paradigms. We show that Actris provides a suitable level of abstraction by proving functional correctness of a variety of examples, including a distributed merge sort, a distributed load-balancing mapper, and a variant of the map-reduce model, using relatively simple specifications. Soundness of Actris is proved using a model of its protocol mechanism in the Iris framework. We mechanised the theory of Actris, together with tactics for symbolic execution of programs, as well as all examples in the paper, in the Coq proof assistant","author_count":4,"contributors":["Hinrichsen, Jonas Kastberg","Bengtson, Jesper","Krebbers, Robbert"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382334"],"event":{"acronym":"POPL","title":"ACM SIGPLAN Symposium on Principles of Programming Languages"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Netherlands"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Delft University of Technology"],"keyword":["Functional Correctness","Message passing","Session types","concurrent programming","separation logics"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"actris session type based reasoning in separation logic","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3371074","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382334","identifier":[{"id":"85079453170","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-475db6ac-7a50-4f01-8c05-1a7e17de8d63"],"keyword":["concurrent programming","functional correctness","message passing","separation logics","session types"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-C4DEE961277F325C1AECC9684ADD8CCE"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3371074","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85391057/3371074.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Programming Logic and Semantics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"nl","key":"delft university of technology","loc":["Netherlands","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Delft University of Technology","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jonas Kastberg","key":"hinrichsenpau","last":"Hinrichsen","name":"Hinrichsen, Jonas Kastberg","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jesper","key":"bengtsonpau","last":"Bengtson","locid":"b2b68ce2-6257-45bf-ab48-dc17d05c885e","name":"Bengtson, Jesper","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Robbert","key":"krebberspau","last":"Krebbers","name":"Krebbers, Robbert","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Philip","key":"wadlerpe